text:
How do you turn the worst mistake of your career into a personal brand win?

@lifeof_jer, CEO of @pocketosai , just gave a masterclass in building in public when it breaks.

Here's the story:

@zuess05 Hot take, but wrong direction.

We scaled to 250 people. The bottleneck was never too many PMs.

It was PMs who couldn't say no to features.

@ThePrimeagen "Codify every rule" is the underrated part.

We spent two weeks writing our internal standards before letting AI touch our codebase.

The teams that skipped that step are still cleaning up.

@arvidkahl The documents folder approach is exactly right.

We learned this the hard way. AI without context builds fast and breaks everything.

Context-first, code-second. Same rule we use for onboarding new engineers.

@_Investinq I've hired 300  people over 9 years.

The best engineers weren't the best coders. They were the ones who could explain what they built.

Communication was always the moat. AI just made it obvious.

@willahmed We went from 50 to 250 people while automating half our workflows.

AI didn't replace headcount. It changed what we hire for.

Less manual ops. More product thinkers.

@wallstengine Ran this evaluation ourselves. Enterprise buyers don't want LLM lock-in.

Whoever owns the process layer wins. 
Not the model layer.

@om_patel5 We coordinate AI across multiple products with 250 people.

49 agents sounds great until agent #37 contradicts agent #12 at 3 AM.

The coordination layer is where everything breaks.

@ujjwalscript Hired over 300 people in 9 years. The ones who stood out never listed skills.

They showed me a problem they solved and what it saved.

That was the whole interview.

@farzyness Same. 250 people, busier than ever.

AI didn't cut the work. 
It raised the bar for what counts as work.

@dee_bosa After the Fiverr acquisition, I watched public e-commerce companies scramble to show growth numbers.

Most of them bought time, not growth.

The ones still standing built products people actually need. Everything else is financial theater.

@rohanpaul_ai I started at 14, buying phone cases for $10 and selling them for $50 on eBay.

No AI. No tools. Just arbitrage and Wi-Fi.

Now imagine a 14-year-old with these tools. The gap between idea and revenue just went from years to hours.

@steipete $852B valuation. Zero profit.

Meanwhile, there are bootstrapped SaaS companies achieving $10M in ARR, 90% margins, and 3 employees.

The most valuable AI companies in 10 years might be the ones nobody is funding today.

@ThePrimeagen Our best engineers don't write more code with AI.

They ship more products.

The metric changed from lines of code to features per week.

@heyibinance We hired an AI consultant at AutoDS last year.

Not to replace people. To make 250 employees do what used to take 400.

The shift isn't "AI does my job." It's "AI makes my job worth 2x more."

@ArthurMacwaters Built AutoDS for 8 years with zero funding.

No pitch deck. No investor meetings. Just customers paying every month.

The "right way" is whichever way keeps you alive long enough to win.

@MatthewBerman We have 100 engineers at AutoDS.

All of them use Claude Code.

The "secret" isn't in the source files. It's in how you structure the prompts that your team feeds it.

@Polymarket $1M/day is wild. 

Building a product people love to try is easy. 
Building one they pay for monthly is a completely different game.

@Pirat_Nation Downloads without retention. The most expensive vanity metric in tech.

We track one number at AutoDS: Do sellers come back tomorrow?

If the answer is no, nothing else matters. Sora had a million users. It never had a million use cases.

@Akintola_steve We had a version of this at AutoDS.

The feature went viral on a forum. Traffic 10x'd overnight. The system couldn't handle it.

That week we stopped celebrating growth and started stress-testing for it. Painful lesson, but it only needs to happen once.

@hthieblot Was exactly this founder at 21.

eBay banned my account. Zero revenue overnight. Took a loan to cover a tax bill I didn't know existed.

Built a mentorship program the same month to survive. That desperation became the muscle that built everything after.

@t_blom Already happening at companies I know.

One founder told me his Claude API bill passed his first engineer's salary 4 months ago.

The question isn't whether it happens. It's whether the ROI justifies it. For most use cases we've tested, it does. By a lot.

@thedealdirector 9 years selling SaaS in e-commerce.

The pitch hasn't changed. "Here's your problem. Here's how we fix it. Here's proof."

What changed: buyers now test 3 AI alternatives before your demo call even starts. If your product doesn't show value in 10 minutes, you lose.

@trikcode Saw this happen with our 100 engineers.

AI made the fast people faster. It made the slow people feel overwhelmed.

The gap didn't shrink. It tripled.

@theo Started AutoDS at 21.

9 years later, 250 employees, exit to a public company.

Being a founder isn't easier. It's just the only option when you're too impatient to wait for permission.

AI compute demand got so intense, we're moving servers to space.

@nvidia-backed Starcloud raised $170M to build an 88,000-satellite constellation for AI data centers.

When the power grid can't keep up, you leave the planet.

@RoundtableSpace The same thing happened in e-commerce 3 years ago.

AI started generating product listings and images at scale.

Sellers who moved first 10x'd their catalog overnight.

@atulkumarzz Tested 6 AI agents for customer support last quarter.

5 needed more babysitting than the humans they replaced.

The one that worked did one thing. But did it perfectly.

@coreyganim The math works.

But we validated every product by selling it first. Then building.

Find 10 people who'll pay before you write a line of code.

@RoundtableSpace 250 people across multiple products.

The bottleneck was never the tools.

It was the context stuck in people's heads that never made it into any system.

@SuryaUthkarsha @ycombinator @realthomasgu @richardzphotoz @jonathanzliu Started my first business at 14. Flipped phones from China on eBay.

21 years later, same energy.

Keep going.

@unusual_whales We track AI prompt usage per team, per week.

Not to monitor. To see who's adapting.

Nicotine isn't a productivity system. Curiosity is.

@swyx Shipped 47 features last year.

Users remember three of them.

The ones that worked on day one.

@tankots We serve sellers in 50  countries.

The #1 request is never "make it smarter."

Always "make it simpler."

@bcherny 100 engineers on my team.

The ones shipping fastest aren't the best coders.

They're the ones who learned to prompt.

@DataChaz The feature most teams sleep on: persistent memory.

We set it up so Claude remembers our pricing logic, competitor data, and product roadmap across sessions.

New hires are onboarded through Claude before talking to a human. Saved us 2 weeks per hire

@ivanburazin This is exactly why we build AI that does the work FOR the user, not AI that gives them more work to do.

Our sellers don't want to create content. They want sales.

The products that win give results, not tools.

@HedgieMarkets Klarna bragged about replacing 700 people with a chatbot.

Then customer satisfaction tanked, and they started quietly rehiring.

We went the other direction. Kept all 250 people, added AI to their workflows. Revenue per employee up 40%.

In February, 3 companies absorbed 83% of all global VC.

@OpenAI raised $110B. @Anthropic closed a $30B round.

If you're building outside that orbit, you're playing a different game.

@OpenAI just backed a $650M startup whose only job is to coordinate AI agents.

Not build models. Not train data. Just coordinate agents.

The bottleneck shifted.

@gregisenberg We run both in production.

Opus for architecture decisions. Codex for rapid iteration.

Choosing one over the other is like choosing between a strategist and a sprinter. You need both on the team.

@gregisenberg We killed our own pricing model twice in 4 years.

Hurt like hell both times. Revenue dipped for 2 months.

Then it grew 3x because we stopped protecting what was comfortable and started pricing for what customers actually wanted.

@gregisenberg 3 years ago, we hired 12 full-time content people.

Today we have 4 people and 6 agents doing 3x the output.

The skill that matters now isn't managing people. It's designing the system where humans and agents actually work together.

@gregisenberg When we scaled from 50 to 250 people, the hardest lesson was that more people don't mean more output.

We restructured 3 teams last year around AI workflows.

Same output. 40% fewer meetings. Zero layoffs. That's the path companies skip.

@startupandrew We tested this with internal tools at AutoDS.

Replaced 3 dashboards with one agent that pulls exactly what each team needs.

Nobody missed the UI. They missed the answers. Now they get those faster.

@jasonlk Saw this firsthand at AutoDS.

We built our moat on data and workflow, not on the model.

The moment your product is just a wrapper around an API, you're one prompt away from being replaced.

@gregisenberg We went from 30 engineers on a feature to 5 engineers plus agents shipping the same thing in half the time.

The bottleneck moved from code to context.

The founders who actually talk to customers every day are winning now.

@rauchg The same thing happened to us.

We replaced 3 internal SaaS tools with custom-built alternatives in just a few weeks. 
Better fit, fraction of the cost.

@JLarky We've had @GitHub go down during critical deploys.

Not once did anyone suggest switching.

When your moat is the entire dev ecosystem, availability becomes a feature request, not a dealbreaker.

@SemiAnalysis_ Seeing this in our own numbers.

We cut AI cost per action by 60%. Usage went up 5x.

Jevons Paradox is the best thing that happened to SaaS margins in a decade.

@garrytan We've been shipping AI-generated features to production for months.

Not patches. Full modules.

The gap between "this is overhyped" and "we're already doing it" is about 6 months.

@Yuchenj_UW We've seen this firsthand, managing 100 engineers.

The best ones use AI to ship 3x faster. The average ones use it to look busy.

AI is a multiplier. It multiplies whatever you already are.

@AlexFinn For SaaS founders this is the real headline: AI cost per user just dropped again.

We've watched our compute costs fall 4x in 12 months while output quality went up.

The margin story for AI-native SaaS keeps getting better.

@ostrisai @Lightricks @ltx_model Fine-tuning used to take a team and a month.

Now it takes one developer and an afternoon.

The barrier to custom AI just disappeared.

@business 10,000 AI chips for one startup.

The AI race isn't about models anymore.

It's about who controls the compute.

@Pirat_Nation Capcom just turned "no AI" into a brand differentiator.

In 2026, "100% human-made" is a premium label.

Smart positioning.

@heyrimsha Most people blame the model when prompts fail.

Karpathy just proved the model wasn't the problem.

The instructions were.

@charliejhills This changes everything about AI coding.

The problem was never intelligence. It was amnesia.

Now it learns how YOU work. That's the real unlock.

@deedydas Anonymity online was always an illusion.

AI just made it obvious.

Your writing style is a fingerprint. Always was.
